        narrative: "Your last day is best spent in the western neighbourhoods, which are close enough together to walk between: Meiji Shrine's forest, the boutiques and side lanes of Omotesando and Harajuku, then Shinjuku for the evening. This is the half of Tokyo that first-time visitors picture, and it packs into a single day without rushing.\n\nA private half-day with a local guide works well here precisely because there is no fixed checklist — you say what you actually want, whether that is vintage clothing in Shimokitazawa, a stationery shop crawl, or somewhere to eat that has no English sign. On a three-day trip this is the day to spend on your own interests rather than the landmarks everyone photographs.\n\nFor the evening, a go-kart run through Shibuya Crossing and past Tokyo Tower is an outright tourist stunt and great fun for it. It is not sightseeing and it does not replace a temple, but as a last night in Tokyo it is hard to beat. You will need a valid international driving permit — arrange that at home, because it cannot be issued in Japan.",
