    routeSummary: "Tokyo → Hakone → Kyoto",
    intro: "The fourth day is what makes Kyoto possible, and only just. The trick is to stop treating Tokyo as your base and start moving in one direction: two days in Tokyo, one night in Hakone, then west to Kyoto on the same line you were already travelling. Odawara, the Shinkansen station for Hakone, sits on the Tokaido line between the two cities, so a Hakone stop costs you almost nothing in extra travel. Doubling back to Tokyo would.\n\nBe clear-eyed about what four days buys you in Kyoto: one afternoon and evening, plus a morning if your flight allows it. That is enough for Fushimi Inari at dusk, a walk through Gion and one very good dinner. It is not enough for Arashiyama, Kinkaku-ji and the eastern temples as well. Trying to fit all of them into a single afternoon is exactly how people end up saying Kyoto was crowded and disappointing — they saw four sites' worth of queues and none of the city between them.\n\nThis route also assumes you fly out of Kansai (Osaka) rather than returning to Tokyo. If your ticket is a Tokyo return, either change it to an open-jaw flight or drop Kyoto entirely and use the three-day route with a spare day. Spending four hours of a four-day trip riding back up the Tokaido line is not a good trade.",
    bestFor: "Travellers who have to see Kyoto on this trip and are willing to change hotels twice to do it — ideally flying in to Tokyo and out of Osaka.",
    quickAnswer: "Four days in Japan works as two nights in Tokyo, one in Hakone, and one in Kyoto: Tokyo, the Hakone loop and an onsen, then the Shinkansen west. It only makes sense on an open-jaw ticket flying home from Osaka. On a Tokyo return, the backtrack eats the fourth day and three days in Tokyo is the better trip.",
