        heading: "Day 3 — Into the caldera, and an onsen night",
        narrative: "Leave Tokyo mid-morning rather than at dawn; Hakone is close enough that an early start buys you little. The classic circuit runs the Hakone Tozan switchback railway up the mountainside, a cable car and then the ropeway over Owakudani, where sulphur vents steam through the hillside and they sell eggs boiled black in the spring water. From the far side, a boat crosses Lake Ashinoko to the red torii gate standing in the shallows below Hakone Shrine — which, on a clear day, has Mount Fuji directly behind it.\n\nThe reason to sleep here rather than day-trip is the evening. Hakone empties after about 5pm when the day-trippers leave, and a ryokan with a hot spring bath, a kaiseki dinner and nothing at all to do afterwards is one of the genuine pleasures of a Japan trip. It also puts you 35 minutes from Odawara for tomorrow's train.\n\nIf you arrive with an afternoon to spare, Hakone has a long craft tradition in yosegi-zaiku, the geometric inlaid woodwork used for those puzzle boxes. A workshop is a better use of two hours than a fourth museum.",
