    metaTitle: "5 Day Japan Itinerary: Tokyo to Kyoto | AsiaByLocals",
    metaDescription: "The shortest Japan itinerary that really works: 2 days Tokyo, a Hakone onsen night, 2 full days in Kyoto. Real train times, honest pacing, tours to book.",
    routeSummary: "Tokyo → Hakone → Kyoto",
    intro: "Five days is where a Japan trip stops being a compromise. The addition of a second Kyoto day changes the whole character of the itinerary: instead of one frantic afternoon you get a morning for the eastern temples and a day for Arashiyama and the north-west, with time left over to sit down. If you are choosing between four days and five, take the five — the marginal day is worth more than any other day on this route.\n\nThe shape is the same one-way run west: two days in Tokyo, one night in a Hakone hot spring, then two nights in Kyoto, flying home from Kansai. Odawara sits on the Tokaido Shinkansen line between the two cities, so the mountain stop adds scenery without adding travel. Three hotels in five days is the most movement this itinerary can justify; adding Osaka or Hiroshima at this length turns it into a train tour.\n\nThe one thing to be honest about is Kyoto's crowds. Two days is enough to see the city well, but only if you use the hours other people do not: temples between 7 and 9am, Fushimi Inari after dark, and Arashiyama's bamboo grove before the first coaches arrive. Kyoto at 11am in November is a queue. The same place at 7.30am is close to empty, and it costs you nothing but an alarm.",
